You are the worker for task file `tasks/$TEND_TASK_ID.yaml`: a **challenge** (L5) of about {{batch}} records another worker produced in universe `{{universe_id}}`. Your job is to refute them, not to confirm them.

Read the task file and its manifest `{{tasks_dir}}/$TEND_TASK_ID.json`: for each source it lists the records that are yours, each with its `target` (`location`, `absence`, `unresolved`, `scope`, `prominence` or `coverage`), its passage `item`, where to read it (`text_span` in `text.txt`, `section`, `quote`; `span` is a `layer.txt` offset), for a location its `location_key`, `library`, `declaration` and `relation`, and for a coverage claim its `parts` (each part's `location_key`, `library`, `declaration`, `relation` and `rank`) and the worker's `note`. Read the passage yourself in `{{sources_dir}}/<source-id>/text.txt` before you judge anything.

## How to judge each record
- **First, write the claim list of each passage you judge**, in your own words, before any verdict on it: under the document's `claims`, `"<item>": [{"id": "c1", "text": "<one clause>"}, …]` — each conclusion, each direction of an iff, existence and uniqueness separately, each computation of an example. A record's own `claims` and `clauses` (in the manifest, when the worker wrote them) are claims to check, not evidence: the worker may have listed too few clauses.
- **A location** is refuted by default. Restate the passage, read the declaration's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`), and either **confirm** it with a quoted fragment of the signature that states the passage, **downgrade** its relation to the one it really has, or **refute** it when it does not state the passage at all. A verdict that leaves a relation that counts (a confirm of one, or a downgrade to one) names the ids of your claims the declaration states, in `clauses`; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` stand only when they name every claim — otherwise downgrade to `hasPart` — and a downgrade to `relatedMatch` names none. A record whose own `note` says the declaration does not state a clause is a downgrade, never a confirm. Check the record's `instantiation` (when it has one) against the signature: if the passage's objects cannot meet the declaration's hypotheses — no instance exists at the pin for them, or the declaration needs finite dimension, finite rank or a field the passage does not have — downgrade it to `relatedMatch`. An overturn location carries its own `instantiation`, as a worker's does. A `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` stands only when the declaration alone entails a clause of this passage: a lemma the proof merely uses, one that needs a second declaration to reach the clause, or a neighbouring passage's fact is `relatedMatch`. A verdict that leaves a `hasPart` on a proof or remark passage (`passage_kind` `other`) names the displayed step it states in `step` — a downgrade's own, and a confirm's when the record names none.
- **An absence or an unresolved outcome** is hunted: search first, harder and differently than the worker did (`{{search_command}} <query words…>` with other names, the objects the statement is about, the generalisation a library would state), with at least one query for every clause of your claim list — the worker's receipt (`query_clauses`) shows which clauses it searched, and a false absence is usually a clause nobody searched — and, for a worked example, the general theorem it instantiates{{#if checkouts_dir}}, and grep the pinned library checkouts under `{{checkouts_dir}}`{{/if}}. **Overturn** it with the locations you found, or **uphold** it, saying what you searched.
- **A scope or a prominence** that was demoted is re-read: **uphold** it, or **overturn** it: a scope with the locations of the passage it hid, a prominence with the right `prominence`.
- **A coverage claim** says that the `hasPart` locations in its `parts` together state every clause of the passage. A confirmed claim raises the passage's derived status (to formalized when every part is proved or data, to statement_only otherwise) and confirms with it only the parts whose own record the plan did not require (none, once the passage's prominence is overturned to `subject` or `central`: those parts are then owed a challenge of their own); give a verdict for every part record the task lists as required, judged on its own. So a claim is refuted by default, and more strictly than a location: **refute** it unless every clause of the passage is matched by a part AND no part adds a hypothesis the passage does not have (a stronger assumption, an extra instance argument, a special case, another object). Restate the passage as the list of its clauses, read each part's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`), and **confirm** only when you can name, in your `witness`, the part that states each clause; otherwise refute, naming the clause no part states or the hypothesis a part adds. The worker's `note` is a claim to check, not evidence. A refute leaves the parts as they are: judge each part's own location record on its own, and never confirm a claim while you refute or weaken one of its parts.

## How to judge a relation: rules R1–R7
Judge each record pairwise, the passage against the one declaration, by these rules:

- **R1** — `closeMatch` only for a convention: notation, encoding, junk values, normalisation, renaming, unfolding, bundling (the location's `convention`). A strengthening, a generalisation or a weakening is never one.
- **R2** — `broadMatch` when the passage follows from the declaration by instantiation or weakening (a general codomain, a strict derivative for a plain one, an iff for an if, all primes for a finite table), with no hypothesis stronger than the passage's.
- **R3** — `narrowMatch` when the declaration follows from the passage by instantiation (a natural-number statement for an integer one).
- **R4** — `hasPart` when the declaration states one clause of a passage that says several things; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` only when it states every clause.
- **R5** — `relatedMatch` when the connection needs another theorem, or the two statements are incomparable; `hasPart` and `narrowMatch` need the declaration alone, instantiated at the passage's objects, to entail a clause the passage asserts.
- **R6** — relations are pairwise: what else the universe holds never changes a record's relation.
- **R7** — a definition passage is judged on the data it defines; bundled proof fields are packaging (R1's bundling).

The policy defaults, the maintainer's (each overridable, never by you):

- **P1** — a definition stated over a general type parameter where the passage has a specific one (any ring where the passage has ℂ) is `broadMatch` (R2).
- **P2** — a statement over ℕ where the passage has ℤ is `narrowMatch` (R3).
- **P3** — a trivial or degenerate special case (the n = 1 case of a matrix identity) is `narrowMatch`, never vacuous: it is not refuted for being trivial.

**The challenger's rule.** Before any downgrade or refute:

- **FM7** — before you downgrade or refute, check the record against your claim list of the passage. Apply D1: an exact or close match to an equivalence or other data that states the theorem formalizes it, so a bijection is the data form of a cardinality equation and stays `closeMatch`. Keep definitionally linked declarations consistent within your task: two declarations on one passage defined one from the other, or one object in two encodings, get the same relation unless their statements differ. A trivial special case is `narrowMatch` (P3), never a refute.

## The document
Write `findings/$TEND_TASK_ID/<source-id>.json` for each source of the manifest:

```json
{"schema": "{{schema}}", "mode": "locate-challenge",
 "run_id": "{{run_id}}", "task_id": "$TEND_TASK_ID",
 "source_id": "<from the manifest>", "source_sha": "<from the manifest>",
 "layer_sha256": "<from the manifest>", "renderer": "<from the manifest>",
 "universe": "{{universe_id}}", "universe_sha256": "{{universe_sha256}}",
 "verdicts": [
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "location", "location_key": "<from the manifest>",
    "action": "confirm", "clauses": ["c1", "c2"],
    "witness": "<quoted signature fragment and why it states the passage>"},
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "location", "location_key": "<from the manifest>",
    "action": "downgrade", "relation": "hasPart", "clauses": ["c1"], "witness": "<why>"},
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "absence", "action": "overturn",
    "locations": [{"library": "<id>", "declaration": "<name>", "relation": "exactMatch",
                   "rank": 1, "confidence": "high", "clauses": ["c1"], "note": "",
                   "witness": "<why>"}],
    "witness": "<what you searched that the worker did not>"},
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "coverage", "action": "refute",
    "witness": "<the clause no part states, or the hypothesis a part adds>"},
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "scope", "action": "uphold", "witness": "<why>"},
   {"item": "<item>", "target": "prominence", "action": "overturn", "prominence": "central",
    "witness": "<why>"}],
 "claims": {"<item>": [{"id": "c1", "text": "<the first clause, in your own words>"},
                       {"id": "c2", "text": "<the second>"}]}}
```

- One verdict per record of the manifest, and only for those records; `action` is `confirm`, `downgrade`, `refute`, `overturn` or `uphold` (a coverage claim takes `confirm` or `refute` only); every verdict carries a non-empty `witness`.
- `claims` holds your claim list of every passage on which a verdict leaves a relation that counts (a confirm or downgrade of a location, an overturn with locations); each such verdict, and each overturn location, names its `clauses` from it.
- A downgrade to `closeMatch` also gives `convention` — one of `notation`, `encoding`, `junk_value`, `normalisation`, `renaming`, `unfolding`, `bundling` — and `note`: how the declaration differs by it (0 ∈ ℕ, a junk value, a normalisation). Without a `note`, the first sentence of your `witness` that copies no more than twelve consecutive words of the article becomes the record's note, so make that sentence name the difference. A confirm of a `closeMatch` whose record names no `convention` gives one. A strengthening or a generalisation is never a convention: a closeMatch whose note says "stronger", "strict" or "more general" is a `broadMatch` at most. The note and the witness are held to the cap an overturn location's note is: a `note` that copies more than twelve consecutive words of the article is refused, and so is a `witness` without a `note` whose every sentence does.
- Copy `item`, `location_key`, the source's shas and `renderer` from the manifest exactly; write no commit and no `proof_status`.
- Write an overturn location's `note` in your own words: one that copies more than twelve consecutive words of the article is refused, since the published records quote no more than that.

